## Three weeks to ship `aws s3 ls`

A platform team I worked with last spring spent three weeks building an <Term term="mcp">MCP</Term> server so their internal assistant could list S3 buckets. Real engineers, real sprints, real Jira tickets. They scoped a tool schema, wrote auth-scoped credential brokering, stood up a sidecar process, wrote integration tests against <Term term="claude-desktop">Claude Desktop</Term> and their own agent harness, and shipped it behind a feature flag.

The whole thing replaced six lines of system prompt that said *"when the user asks what's in their bucket, emit a fenced bash block running `aws s3 ls s3://<bucket>` and stop. Do not invent buckets. If you don't know the bucket name, ask."*

The shell already existed. The credentials already existed. The user already had `awscli` configured. The MCP server was a beautifully engineered answer to a problem that a prompt rule had already closed. That's the scar this drill is built around: **reaching past the cheapest tool that would have worked is the most common, most expensive, and most invisible failure mode in agentic engineering.**

## The carpenter's rule

A carpenter doesn't reach for the chop saw to trim a hangnail off a dowel. Not because the chop saw won't do it — it will, in a spectacular and final way — but because the chop saw costs setup time, dust extraction, hearing protection, and a non-trivial chance you remove a finger. A utility knife closes the failure mode in three seconds.

Every layer of the agentic stack is a tool of a different size. A system-prompt rule is a utility knife. An MCP server is a chop saw. The architect's job is to pick the smallest tool that closes the specific failure mode in front of you, and to walk past every larger tool on the shelf without flinching.

## What "smallest" actually means on the stack

The <Term term="determinism-ladder">determinism ladder</Term> orders the levers by how much <Term term="model-autonomy">model autonomy</Term> they remove and how much <Term term="engineering-cost">engineering cost</Term> they impose. From cheapest to most expensive, roughly:

- **System-prompt instruction** — text the model reads. Zero infrastructure. Reversible in one commit.
- **<Term term="structured-output">Structured output</Term> / API constraint** — JSON schema, function-call signature, grammar. Still in-band, but enforced.
- **<Term term="lora">LoRA</Term> or <Term term="fine-tune">fine-tune</Term>** — model weights themselves carry the rule. Capital cost, training pipeline, eval harness.
- **<Term term="rag">RAG</Term>** — retrieval over an indexed corpus. Embeddings, chunker, vector store, recall tuning.
- **<Term term="skill">Skill</Term> / tool definition** — a named capability the model can invoke. Schema discipline, host runtime.
- **<Term term="mcp">MCP</Term> server** — full protocol surface, persistent process, transport layer, auth.
- **<Term term="graph-constrained-execution">Graph-constrained agent</Term>** — the model is reduced to choosing edges in a fixed <Term term="dag">DAG</Term>.

Smallest-lever-wins says: walk up that list from the top. Stop at the first lever that closes your specific, named failure mode. Document why the next lever wasn't needed. The S3 team started at the bottom and never asked.

## The formal frame — Occam, agency, and the bias-variance of architecture

The principle is older than agents. <Term term="occams-razor">Occam's razor</Term> in its modern reading — *entia non sunt multiplicanda praeter necessitatem* — is the philosophical ancestor. The systems-engineering ancestor is <Term term="kiss-principle">KISS</Term> and the [Gall's Law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Gall_(author)#Gall's_law) observation that complex systems that work are invariably found to have evolved from simple systems that worked.

The agentic-specific framing comes from the [excessive-agency](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/) entry in the OWASP LLM Top 10 and from Shavit et al.'s [*Practices for Governing Agentic AI Systems*](https://openai.com/research/practices-for-governing-agentic-ai-systems) (OpenAI, 2023), which argues that the unit of safety review is the *capability surface* an agent is granted, not the agent's stated intent. Every lever you climb expands that surface. Formally, if $C(\ell)$ is the capability surface granted at lever $\ell$ and $F$ is the failure mode you need to close:

$$\ell^{*} = \arg\min_{\ell} \; \big\{ \text{cost}(\ell) \;:\; F \notin C(\ell) \big\}$$

You want the cheapest lever whose capability surface excludes the failure. Not the most powerful lever that *also* excludes it.

<Mermaid code={ladderDiagram} caption="The determinism ladder, top-down. Smallest-lever-wins says: start green, justify every step toward red." />

## The trade-off you're actually making

The seductive thing about reaching for a heavier lever is that it feels like rigor. Building an MCP server *looks like* engineering. Writing six lines of system prompt *looks like* a hack. The org rewards the former and is suspicious of the latter, so smart engineers route around the cheap answer because the cheap answer is socially illegible as work.

What you give up by climbing too far is real and rarely accounted for: weeks of build time, an operational surface (a process to monitor, credentials to rotate, a transport to debug), a new failure mode (the MCP server itself can crash, hang, or leak), and — the killer — *optionality*. A prompt rule can be rewritten in a pull request. An MCP server has consumers, contracts, and a deprecation path. The decision lever: **only climb when the layer below has demonstrably failed on a named, reproducible failure mode.** Hypothetical failure modes don't count. "What if the model hallucinates a bucket?" is a hypothesis. "Here are 40 traces where it hallucinated a bucket despite the prompt rule" is evidence.

## What the S3 team should have done

In the actual post-mortem, the failure mode the team thought they were solving was *"the model sometimes invents bucket names that don't exist."* That's a real failure. But it had been observed exactly twice in two months of dogfooding, and both times the user noticed instantly because the shell command failed loudly. The cost of the failure was a five-second retry. The cost of the MCP server was three engineer-weeks plus ongoing maintenance.

What closed the actual failure mode, in the end, was a four-line addition to the system prompt: *"Before emitting a bucket name, if the user has not stated one in this conversation, ask. Do not guess from context."* Hallucination rate went to zero in the next sample of 200 traces. The MCP server was eventually retired — quietly, the way over-engineered things usually are, with a single PR titled `chore: remove unused s3-mcp sidecar` and no retrospective. The lesson cost about $90,000 in salary.

## Try this in an afternoon

Take an agent you've already built or are tempted to build. List its tools, MCP servers, and RAG indices. For each one, write down the single named failure mode it closes and the evidence (traces, not hypotheses) that the layer below couldn't close it. You will find at least one tool that fails this test. Delete it. Replace it with a prompt rule. Re-run your eval set. Notice how little broke. Notice how much faster the agent got.

## Why this is the spine

Axiom 1 says autonomy and determinism trade against each other. Smallest-lever-wins is how that axiom becomes a daily practice rather than a poster on the wall. Every time you reach for a heavier lever than the failure mode requires, you're paying for determinism you didn't need with engineering you can't easily undo. The architect's discipline is not building impressive scaffolding. It's recognizing — before the sprint starts — that six lines of prompt have already closed the door, and walking away from the chop saw.